      "2": {
        "attributes": {
          "font-size": {
            "type": "number",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Convert degrees to radians"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 69.63587951660156,
          "width": 195,
          "x": -78.24371337890625,
          "y": 494.1724548339844
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 0
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [],
        "name": "Comment"
      },
      "20": {
        "attributes": {
          "font-size": {
            "type": "number",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "INVERSE TRIGONOMETRIC FUNTIONS\n\nThe known functions: sine, cosin and tangent have corresponding inverse functions, namely asin, acos and atan (arcsine, arcosine and arctangent). They are used to obtain an angle from any of the angle's trigonometric functions. Let us see an example of these by recovering the input angle. For the sake of simplicity we will use degrees as our input. Notice that a conversion to radians is performed since sin, cos and tan expect that.\n\nAlso note that there are some further constrains for asin, acos and atan, namely that:\n\nasin(y) = x for -90 <= x <= 90\nacos(y) = x for 0 <= x <= 180\natan(y) = x for -90 <= x <= 90\n\nTo fully understand this, let's think what does it then mean to perform asin(y) = x? Well, it simply means the following question: what angle x is equal to y when taking its sine? Or sin(x) = y\n\nThis gives us a clue as well of what are valid inputs for asin() acos() and atan(). Since sin(x) will always give us values from -1 to 1, then possible values for asin() are only in that range. The same reasoning applies to the other inverse functions. This is the reason for example why the possible output values for our inverse functions are those described above, since sin(x) = -1 is true when x = -90 degrees and sin(x) = 1 is true when x = 90 degrees.\n\nFurthermore, this constrain is the case because otherwise there will be an infinite number of solutions for this functions, given that 0, 360, 720, etc degrees represent in essence the same angle. The constrain makes sure that there's always one single result, consistent with the equation."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 552.14453125,
          "width": 711.5283203125,
          "x": -314.84368896484375,
          "y": -77.46417236328125
        },
